Bug 1: saturation to +127 is written as 126 — wrong, should be 127.

Let me check the extended computation. `{a[7],a}` - replication concatenation. `{a[7],a}` is 9 bits: sign bit then a. That's sign extension, correct. Sum extended is 9 bits signed, range -256..254, fine.

Saturation: if extended > 127 => sum <= 126 (bug, should be 127). if extended < -128 => sum <= -128, correct.

Actually wait, when extended > 127 we want 127. Note 127 as signed 8-bit is 8'h7F. Writing `sum <= 127` in a signed context: 127 fits. Fine.

Let me fix. Also potential issue: `extended` declared as wire signed [8:0], concatenation `{a[7],a}` is unsigned but assigned to signed wire, then addition of two 9-bit... {a[7],a} as unsigned 9-bit added: in Verilog, both operands unsigned -> unsigned addition, then result assigned to signed wire - bit pattern same. Comparison `extended > 127` — extended is signed, 127 is signed integer -> signed comparison. Good. `extended < -128` signed. Good.

So the only bug is 126 -> 127. Let me write clean code.

Let me use explicit wider signed arithmetic: `wire signed [8:0] sum_ext = $signed(a) + $signed(b);` Actually a is already signed, addition of two 8-bit signed gives 8-bit result in Verilog (context-determined)... better to extend. Let me use:

```
logic signed [8:0] sum_ext;
always_comb sum_ext = $signed(a) + $signed(b);
```

But $signed(a) is 8-bit, sum of two 8-bit signed is 9-bit? No — the result width is max of operand widths = 8, assigned to 9-bit LHS gives sign extension. Hmm, that would overflow before extension. Actually in Verilog, in an assignment the expression is evaluated in the context of the LHS width in some cases... For arithmetic, the self-determined size rules: the addition is context-determined, so LHS width propagates to operands. With `logic signed [8:0] sum_ext = a + b;` — LHS width 9 propagates, so a and b are sign-extended to 9 bits, result 9 bits. Good.

Safest explicit: `wire signed [8:0] sum_ext = {a[7], a} + {b[7], b};` — as before, works bit-wise.

Let me write final:

```
module signed_saturating_add(
  input clk,
  input rst,
  input signed [7:0] a,
  input signed [7:0] b,
  output reg signed [7:0] sum
);
  wire signed [8:0] sum_ext = {a[7], a} + {b[7], b};

  always @(posedge clk) begin
    if (rst)
      sum <= 8'sd0;
    else if (sum_ext > 9'sd127)
      sum <= 8'sd127;
    else if (sum_ext < -9'sd128)
      sum <= -8'sd128;
    else
      sum <= sum_ext[7:0];
  end
endmodule
```

Note `-9'sd128` is not valid? 9'sd128 = 128 which as 9-bit signed is out of range (max 255 for... signed 9-bit max 255? no, signed 9-bit range -256..255, so 128 is fine). -9'sd128 = -128, fine within -256..255. OK. Actually `9'sd128` — 128 fits in signed 9-bit (max 255). Fine.

But careful: comparison `sum_ext > 9'sd127`: sum_ext is signed 9-bit, so ok. Alternatively use `> 127`.

Let me use `if (sum_ext > 127)` and `if (sum_ext < -128)`. Both compare signed since sum_ext is signed. Good.

